1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,611
Debt snowball, or debt avalanche? People argue about this constantly.

2
00:00:05,031 --> 00:00:08,415
So let's just run the numbers, and see which one actually wins.

3
00:00:08,835 --> 00:00:10,646
Here's our example. Three debts.

4
00:00:10,806 --> 00:00:14,277
A credit card: $3,200 at 24%.

5
00:00:14,437 --> 00:00:17,107
A personal loan: $800 at 12%.

6
00:00:17,267 --> 00:00:20,016
And a car loan: $9,000 at 7%.

7
00:00:20,436 --> 00:00:25,894
The minimums add up to $376. We'll put $900 a month toward debt.

8
00:00:26,314 --> 00:00:30,745
Both methods pay every minimum. The only question is where the extra goes.

9
00:00:31,515 --> 00:00:35,912
First, the avalanche. Extra money goes to the highest interest rate first.

10
00:00:36,332 --> 00:00:37,813
So the credit card is first.

11
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:39,999
Then the personal loan, then the car.

12
00:00:40,419 --> 00:00:42,312
The credit card is gone in month six.

13
00:00:42,732 --> 00:00:44,494
The personal loan, month seven.

14
00:00:44,914 --> 00:00:47,323
And the car loan, month sixteen. Debt-free.

15
00:00:48,093 --> 00:00:52,940
Now the snowball. Extra money goes to the smallest balance first, no matter the rate.

16
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:55,221
The personal loan is gone in month two.

17
00:00:55,641 --> 00:00:57,263
The credit card, month seven.

18
00:00:57,683 --> 00:01:00,656
And the car loan, month sixteen. The same finish line.

19
00:01:01,426 --> 00:01:02,644
So, which one wins?

20
00:01:03,064 --> 00:01:06,316
Avalanche: $805 in total interest.

21
00:01:06,736 --> 00:01:08,648
Snowball: $847.

22
00:01:09,068 --> 00:01:12,097
The difference: $42. Over sixteen months.

23
00:01:12,517 --> 00:01:16,374
But look at this. With the avalanche, your first win comes in month six.

24
00:01:16,794 --> 00:01:18,509
With the snowball, it's month two.

25
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:21,133
And the best method is the one you don't quit.

26
00:01:21,553 --> 00:01:29,405
If you need quick wins to stay motivated, the snowball's extra cost is small. If you're steady and want the least interest, go avalanche.

27
00:01:29,825 --> 00:01:39,970
The real win was the $900 a month. Minimums only: about 4½ years and $3,393 in interest.

28
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:46,441
The Paycheck Planner in the description runs both methods on your own debts, and gives you your debt-free date.

29
00:01:46,861 --> 00:01:49,617
Subscribe for more money math, with real numbers.
